- surfaces/table: grip drag-to-reorder with a jade drop line (§5.4). The verb
  has two homes already (menu, Alt+Arrows), so the drag is the gesture half of
  law 8, not a missing capability. Wait for M9's drop-line plugin rather than
  building a second one.
- surfaces/table: while the pointer is inside a nested table's hover zone the
  outer table shows no chrome at all — one hover owner, and the inner reveal
  holds it (`nestedCellKeepsReveal`, PR #495, load-bearing at every depth).
  If writers want outer-row verbs reachable without first leaving the inner
  zone, that is a design change to the one-owner model and needs a human
  ruling before any code.
- surfaces/table: the table's own verbs (header row, placement, reset widths)
  have no keyboard twin. §4 gives the table Tab, Alt+Arrows, Enter, and Esc and
  no door to its menu; a twin needs a design call, not an invented binding.
